{
  "image_name": "0a3ff60a306c98ebe69819b5246fb1e4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed link between Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) infection in the periphery and inflammatory damage in the central nervous system. On the left, EBV infects naïve B cells through surface receptors and drives a germinal-center–like reaction, producing EBV-positive B-cell populations. One branch shows a type I memory-like B cell carrying an episome and expressing mainly EBNA1, which appears relatively immune evasive. Another branch shows type III infected B cells with broader viral protein expression, exosomes, and defective cytotoxic T-cell control, alongside possible lytic reactivation.\n\nThese infected or activated peripheral cells seem to promote inflammatory B cells and autoreactive T cells. Signals near the blood vessel, including inflammatory mediators such as IL-6, OPN, and CXCR4, are associated with movement across the barrier into the CNS. On the right, the CNS side shows astrocytes, microglia, neurons, and EBNA1-reactive immune cells. The image suggests that infiltrating B and T cells may contribute to CNS trafficking, autoreactivity, and molecular mimicry, with a highlighted interaction between EBNA1 reactivity and myelin-related glial/neuronal targets. Additional cytokines, exosomes, inflamed astrocytes, microglial activation, and deficient T-cell control reinforce a broader inflammatory cascade.",
